/* Shortcut Press — Professional Document Formatting guide content (generated by tools/site/build.mjs). */
window.SP_GUIDE = {"id":"document-formatting","slug":"professional-document-formatting","title":"Professional Document Formatting","software":"Microsoft Word or Google Docs","apps":["Word","Docs"],"intro":"Format long documents so they stay consistent and easy to update: styles, headings, automatic tables of contents, page layout, and reusable templates. Every lesson covers both Microsoft Word and Google Docs; where the steps differ, both are shown.","platformNote":"Shortcuts are shown for Windows and ChromeOS. On a Mac, use Cmd in place of Ctrl; Word for Mac also uses Cmd+Option in place of Ctrl+Alt.","files":[{"name":"report-draft.docx","title":"Report draft","desc":"A short report typed with no styles at all, for the styles and headings lessons.","size":"2 KB"},{"name":"handbook-draft.docx","title":"Handbook draft","desc":"A longer document with several chapters, for the table of contents and page layout lessons.","size":"3 KB"},{"name":"letter-draft.docx","title":"Letter draft","desc":"A one-page letter to turn into a reusable template.","size":"2 KB"}],"shortcuts":[{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+1","action":"Apply Heading 1","app":"Word for Windows and Docs"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+2","action":"Apply Heading 2","app":"Word for Windows and Docs"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+3","action":"Apply Heading 3","app":"Word for Windows and Docs"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Shift+N","action":"Apply the Normal style","app":"Word for Windows","practice":false,"note":"Your browser reserves this shortcut for a new private window."},{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+0","action":"Apply Normal text","app":"Docs"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Enter","action":"Insert a page break","app":"Word and Docs"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Shift+8","action":"Show or hide formatting marks","app":"Word for Windows"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Space","action":"Clear character formatting","app":"Word for Windows"},{"keys":"Ctrl+\\","action":"Clear formatting","app":"Docs"},{"keys":"F9","action":"Update the selected field, such as a table of contents","app":"Word for Windows"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Alt+Shift+S","action":"Open the Styles pane","app":"Word for Windows"},{"keys":"Ctrl+B","action":"Bold","app":"Word and Docs"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Z","action":"Undo","app":"Word and Docs"}],"lessons":[{"id":"styles","title":"Styles","summary":"Apply built-in styles, change a style once so every matching paragraph updates, create your own, and see why formatting by hand causes problems.","diagram":{"type":"page","variant":"styles","title":"The same page formatted by hand and with styles"},"steps":[{"title":"Why styles beat manual formatting","body":"<p>Making a heading bold and 16 point by hand works once. In a 40-page document it means hundreds of separate changes, inconsistencies creep in, and the program can't tell headings from body text. A <strong>style</strong> is a named set of formatting (font, size, spacing, color) applied in one step. Change the style and every paragraph using it updates.</p>"},{"title":"Apply styles","body":"<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> click in a paragraph and pick a style from the <strong>Home</strong> tab's Styles gallery (Normal, Heading 1, Heading 2…).</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> click in a paragraph and pick from the style drop-down on the toolbar, which shows <em>Normal text</em> by default.</li></ul>\n<p>In both programs, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>1</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>2</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>3</kbd> apply Heading 1 to 3. For body text, use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>0</kbd> in Google Docs; in Word, pick Normal from the gallery.</p>"},{"title":"Change a style everywhere","body":"<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> right-click the style in the gallery and choose <strong>Modify</strong>. Set the font, size, color and spacing (use <em>Format &gt; Paragraph</em> in that dialog for space before and after), then select OK. Every paragraph with that style updates.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> format one heading the way you want, keep the cursor in it, open the style drop-down, point at the style (for example Heading 1) and choose <strong>Update 'Heading 1' to match</strong>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Create your own style (Word)","body":"<p>In Word, format a paragraph the way you want, open the Styles gallery's <em>More</em> arrow and choose <strong>Create a Style</strong>, then give it a name such as <em>Note</em>. Open the full Styles pane with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd> to see and manage every style.</p>\n<p>Google Docs has a fixed set of styles (Normal text, Title, Subtitle and Heading 1 to 6). You can change how each looks but can't add new names. To reuse your settings in new documents, choose <strong>Format &gt; Paragraph styles &gt; Options &gt; Save as my default styles</strong>.</p>"},{"title":"Remove stray manual formatting","body":"<p>Manual formatting sits on top of a style and stops it updating. Select the text and clear it: <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Space</kbd> clears character formatting in Word, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>\\</kbd> (or <strong>Format &gt; Clear formatting</strong>) clears formatting in Google Docs. Then apply the right style.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You change Heading 1 to a new font using Modify (Word) or Update to match (Docs). What happens?","options":["Only the selected heading changes","Every Heading 1 in the document changes","Every heading level changes","Nothing until you save"],"answer":1,"explain":"Changing the style updates every paragraph that uses it."},{"q":"Which shortcut applies Heading 2 in both Word for Windows and Google Docs?","options":["Ctrl+2","Ctrl+Alt+2","Ctrl+Shift+2","Alt+2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ctrl+Alt with a number applies that heading level in both programs."},{"q":"Can you add a new named style in Google Docs?","options":["Yes, from the style drop-down","No; Docs has a fixed set you can restyle","Only in Pageless mode","Only for headings"],"answer":1,"explain":"Google Docs lets you change how its built-in styles look, but not add new style names."}],"exercise":{"title":"Style the report","task":"<p>Open <strong>report-draft.docx</strong>. The title and section headings were made bold by hand. Apply Title to the first line, Heading 1 to each section heading and Heading 2 to each sub-heading, then change Heading 1 to a different font so every section heading updates at once.</p>","files":["report-draft.docx"],"solution":["Open the file (Google Docs: <strong>File &gt; Open &gt; Upload</strong>).","Select all with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd> and clear manual formatting (Word <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Space</kbd>, Docs <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>\\</kbd>).","Click in the first line and apply <strong>Title</strong>. Click in each section heading (lines such as “1. Background”) and press Ctrl+Alt+1; click in each sub-heading (such as “Scope”) and press Ctrl+Alt+2.","Word: right-click Heading 1 in the Styles gallery &gt; Modify, pick a new font, select OK. Google Docs: reformat one Heading 1, then choose Update 'Heading 1' to match. All section headings change together."]}},{"id":"headings-structure","title":"Headings and structure","summary":"Use heading levels to give a document a clear outline, move through it by its headings, and number headings consistently.","diagram":{"type":"page","variant":"headings","title":"Heading levels build an outline"},"steps":[{"title":"Plan the levels","body":"<p>Heading 1 is for chapters or main sections, Heading 2 for sections inside them, and Heading 3 for sub-sections. Don't skip a level, such as going from Heading 1 straight to Heading 3. Screen readers, tables of contents and the navigation panes all rely on this outline.</p>"},{"title":"Move through a long document","body":"<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> <strong>View &gt; Navigation Pane</strong> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>F</kbd>) lists every heading on the <em>Headings</em> tab. Click one to jump there; drag a heading to move the whole section, text and all.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> <strong>View &gt; Show outline</strong> lists headings down the left. Click one to jump there.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Numbered headings","body":"<p><strong>Word</strong> can number headings automatically (1, 1.1, 1.2, 2…). Click in a Heading 1, choose <strong>Home &gt; Multilevel List</strong>, and pick a list style that shows \"Heading 1, Heading 2\" in its preview. Numbers update when you move or add sections.</p>\n<p><strong>Google Docs</strong> doesn't link list numbering to heading styles. If you need numbered headings there, type the numbers as part of the heading text and check them after moving sections.</p>"},{"title":"Keep headings with their text","body":"<p>Headings shouldn't sit alone at the bottom of a page. In Word, the built-in heading styles have <em>Keep with next</em> switched on (Modify &gt; Format &gt; Paragraph &gt; Line and Page Breaks). In Google Docs, select the heading and use <strong>Format &gt; Line &amp; paragraph spacing &gt; Keep with next</strong>.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which is a problem in a document outline?","options":["Heading 1 followed by Heading 2","Heading 2 followed by Heading 2","Heading 1 followed directly by Heading 3","Heading 3 followed by Heading 1"],"answer":2,"explain":"Skipping from Heading 1 to Heading 3 leaves a gap in the outline."},{"q":"In Word, what happens when you drag a heading in the Navigation Pane?","options":["Only the heading moves","The heading and its whole section move","The heading is copied","The heading level changes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Navigation Pane moves the heading together with everything under it."}],"exercise":{"title":"Reorganize the handbook","task":"<p>Open <strong>handbook-draft.docx</strong>, apply heading styles to its chapters and sections, open the navigation pane (Word) or outline (Google Docs), and move the chapter “Equipment” so it comes before “Working hours”.</p>","files":["handbook-draft.docx"],"solution":["Apply Heading 1 to each chapter title (lines starting “Chapter”) and Heading 2 to each section inside a chapter.","Word: View &gt; Navigation Pane, then drag “Chapter 3: Equipment” above “Chapter 2: Working hours”. The chapter moves with all its text.","Google Docs: View &gt; Show outline to check the structure. To move a chapter, select its heading and text, cut, and paste it in the new position.","Check the outline lists Welcome, Equipment, Working hours, then the remaining chapters."]}},{"id":"table-of-contents","title":"Automatic tables of contents","summary":"Insert a table of contents built from your headings, choose how many levels it shows, and update it after you edit.","diagram":{"type":"page","variant":"toc","title":"A table of contents is built from the headings"},"steps":[{"title":"Insert a table of contents","body":"<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> put the cursor where it should go (usually after the title page), then <strong>References &gt; Table of Contents</strong> and pick <em>Automatic Table 1</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> <strong>Insert &gt; Table of contents</strong>, then choose a style with page numbers or one with blue links.</li></ul>\n<p>The table lists every paragraph that uses a heading style, which is why styles come first.</p>"},{"title":"Update after editing","body":"<p>A table of contents doesn't change by itself when you edit.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> click in it, then choose <strong>References &gt; Update Table</strong> (or press <kbd>F9</kbd>), pick <em>Update entire table</em>, and select OK.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> click in it and select the refresh button that appears beside it (<em>Update table of contents</em>).</li></ul>\n<p>Never type corrections directly into a table of contents; they're lost at the next update. Fix the heading instead.</p>"},{"title":"Choose how many levels appear","body":"<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> <strong>References &gt; Table of Contents &gt; Custom Table of Contents</strong>, then set <em>Show levels</em> (for example 2 to show Heading 1 and 2 only).</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> click in the table of contents and use the options panel that opens (<em>More options</em>) to choose which heading levels to include.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Page numbers in Google Docs","body":"<p>Google Docs documents can be in <em>Pages</em> or <em>Pageless</em> format (<strong>File &gt; Page setup</strong>). Page numbers in a table of contents and in headers and footers only appear in Pages format. For a document that will be printed or sent as a PDF, use Pages.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does an automatic table of contents get its entries from?","options":["Bold text","Paragraphs with heading styles","The first line of each page","Text in larger fonts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tables of contents are built from heading styles, so text that's only bold won't appear."},{"q":"You fixed a typo directly in the table of contents. What happens at the next update?","options":["The fix is kept","The fix is lost, because entries are rebuilt from the headings","The heading is updated to match","An error appears"],"answer":1,"explain":"Updating rebuilds the table from the headings, so correct the heading itself."}],"exercise":{"title":"Add contents to the handbook","task":"<p>In the styled handbook from the previous lesson, insert a table of contents after the title, showing two heading levels. Then rename one chapter heading and update the table.</p>","files":["handbook-draft.docx"],"solution":["Click at the start of the line after the title and insert a page break (Ctrl+Enter), then move the cursor back onto the empty first page.","Word: References &gt; Table of Contents &gt; Custom Table of Contents, Show levels 2, OK. Google Docs: Insert &gt; Table of contents, then choose the levels in the panel that opens.","Rename “Chapter 4: Leave” to “Chapter 4: Time off”.","Word: click in the table, press F9, choose Update entire table. Google Docs: select the update button beside the table. The new name appears."]}},{"id":"page-layout","title":"Page layout","summary":"Margins, orientation, page and section breaks, headers and footers, and page numbers that start where you want them to.","diagram":{"type":"page","variant":"layout","title":"Sections let one document mix layouts"},"steps":[{"title":"Margins, orientation and size","body":"<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> the <strong>Layout</strong> tab has <em>Margins</em>, <em>Orientation</em> and <em>Size</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> <strong>File &gt; Page setup</strong> sets orientation, paper size and margins (in Pages format).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Page breaks and section breaks","body":"<p>A <strong>page break</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Enter</kbd> in both programs) starts the next text on a new page. Never press Enter repeatedly to push text down.</p>\n<p>A <strong>section break</strong> also lets the next part have its own layout, such as a landscape page for a wide table, or different headers.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> <strong>Layout &gt; Breaks &gt; Next Page</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> <strong>Insert &gt; Break &gt; Section break (next page)</strong>. In File &gt; Page setup, choose <em>Apply to: This section</em> to change only that section.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Headers, footers and page numbers","body":"<ul><li><strong>Word:</strong> <strong>Insert &gt; Header</strong> or <strong>Footer</strong>, and <strong>Insert &gt; Page Number</strong>. In the Header &amp; Footer tab, tick <em>Different First Page</em> to keep the cover page clean. To start numbering at 1 in a later section, choose <em>Page Number &gt; Format Page Numbers &gt; Start at</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> <strong>Insert &gt; Headers &amp; footers</strong>, and <strong>Insert &gt; Page numbers</strong>. In the header's Options, tick <em>Different first page</em>; Page numbers options let you choose where numbering starts.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Different headers in different sections","body":"<p>Each section can have its own header. In Word, open the header of the later section and turn off <strong>Link to Previous</strong> in the Header &amp; Footer tab before changing it; otherwise the change also applies to earlier sections. Google Docs shows a <em>Link to previous</em> checkbox in the header of each section after the first.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You need one landscape page in a portrait document. What do you insert before and after it?","options":["Page breaks","Section breaks (next page)","Column breaks","Several empty paragraphs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Section breaks let each part have its own orientation; page breaks don't."},{"q":"What does turning off Link to Previous do in Word?","options":["Deletes the header","Lets this section's header differ from the previous section's","Removes page numbers","Joins two sections"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unlinking means changes to this header no longer apply to the section before it."}],"exercise":{"title":"Lay out the handbook","task":"<p>In the handbook, keep the title page free of headers and page numbers, put page numbers in the footer of every other page, and make the “Appendix: Contact sheet” page landscape.</p>","files":["handbook-draft.docx"],"solution":["Insert a footer page number (Word: Insert &gt; Page Number &gt; Bottom of Page; Google Docs: Insert &gt; Page numbers).","Tick Different First Page (Word, in the Header &amp; Footer tab) or Different first page (Docs, header Options) so the title page has no number.","Click at the start of “Appendix: Contact sheet” and insert a section break (Word: Layout &gt; Breaks &gt; Next Page; Docs: Insert &gt; Break &gt; Section break (next page)).","With the cursor in the appendix: Word Layout &gt; Orientation &gt; Landscape (it applies to the current section); Docs File &gt; Page setup, Apply to: This section, Landscape, OK."]}},{"id":"templates","title":"Reusable templates","summary":"Save a finished layout as a template and start new documents from it so every report or letter begins with the same structure.","diagram":{"type":"page","variant":"template","title":"One template, many new documents"},"steps":[{"title":"What goes into a template","body":"<p>A template holds everything you'd otherwise set up again: styles, margins, headers and footers, a logo or letterhead, and placeholder text telling the writer what goes where. Remove anything specific to one document before saving it as a template.</p>"},{"title":"Save a Word template","body":"<p>In Word, choose <strong>File &gt; Save As</strong> and pick <strong>Word Template (*.dotx)</strong> as the file type. Word suggests its <em>Custom Office Templates</em> folder. To start a new document from it, go to <strong>File &gt; New</strong> and open the <em>Personal</em> tab (or double-click the .dotx file). Each new document is a copy; the template itself isn't changed.</p>"},{"title":"Templates in Google Docs","body":"<p>Google Docs has a template gallery (<strong>File &gt; New &gt; From template gallery</strong>). Organizations using Google Workspace can add their own templates to it. On a personal account, keep a master document named clearly (for example “TEMPLATE – Letter”) and start each new one with <strong>File &gt; Make a copy</strong>. Your saved default styles (Format &gt; Paragraph styles &gt; Options &gt; Save as my default styles) apply to new documents.</p>"},{"title":"Placeholder text that guides the writer","body":"<p>Put short bracketed instructions where content goes, such as [Recipient name] or [One-paragraph summary], styled with the right style already. The writer replaces them and the formatting stays correct.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which file type saves a reusable Word template?","options":[".docx",".dotx",".pdf",".rtf"],"answer":1,"explain":"Word templates use the .dotx format; new documents created from them are .docx."},{"q":"On a personal Google account, what's the simplest way to reuse a layout?","options":["Add it to the template gallery","Keep a master document and use File > Make a copy","Download it as .dotx","Use Pageless format"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adding to the template gallery is for Workspace organizations; Make a copy works for everyone."}],"exercise":{"title":"Turn the letter into a template","task":"<p>Open <strong>letter-draft.docx</strong>, apply styles to the sender block, date, greeting and body, replace the specific names and dates with bracketed placeholders, and save it as a template (Word) or a clearly named master copy (Google Docs). Then create a new letter from it.</p>","files":["letter-draft.docx"],"solution":["Apply Normal text to the body and a heading or Title style to the sender's name; set the margins you want.","Replace the recipient, date and subject with placeholders such as [Recipient name], [Date] and [Subject].","Word: File &gt; Save As &gt; Word Template (*.dotx). Then File &gt; New &gt; Personal and open it; the new letter opens as an untitled .docx.","Google Docs: rename the document “TEMPLATE – Letter”, then File &gt; Make a copy for each new letter."]}},{"id":"word-and-docs","title":"Word and Google Docs side by side","summary":"Where the steps differ between the two programs, and how to move documents between them without losing formatting.","diagram":{"type":"compare","title":"Where Word and Google Docs differ","left":{"title":"Microsoft Word","items":["Styles gallery and Styles pane","Create new named styles","Multilevel List for numbered headings","References > Update Table","Layout > Breaks","Save as Word Template (.dotx)"]},"right":{"title":"Google Docs","items":["Style drop-down on the toolbar","Fixed set of styles to restyle","Type heading numbers by hand","Update button beside the table","Insert > Break","Master copy and Make a copy"]}},"steps":[{"title":"Same ideas, different places","body":"<p>Both programs use the same building blocks: styles, heading levels, an automatic table of contents, section breaks, and headers and footers. Word puts most of them in ribbon tabs (Home, Insert, Layout, References); Google Docs puts them in menus (Format, Insert, File &gt; Page setup).</p>"},{"title":"Moving documents between them","body":"<ul><li><strong>Open a Word file in Google Docs:</strong> <strong>File &gt; Open &gt; Upload</strong>, or upload to Google Drive and open with Google Docs.</li>\n<li><strong>Save a Google Doc for Word:</strong> <strong>File &gt; Download &gt; Microsoft Word (.docx)</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Headings, the Title style, page breaks, tables of contents, and headers and footers carry across. Custom Word styles become plain formatting in Google Docs, because Docs can't add new style names. Check the table of contents and update it after converting.</p>"},{"title":"A quick check before sending","body":"<ul><li>Show formatting marks (Word <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>8</kbd>) to spot empty paragraphs used as spacing.</li>\n<li>Check the navigation pane or outline for skipped heading levels.</li>\n<li>Update the table of contents.</li>\n<li>Export a PDF (Word: File &gt; Save As &gt; PDF; Docs: File &gt; Download &gt; PDF document) and page through it.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to a custom Word style called “Note” when the file is opened in Google Docs?","options":["It appears in the style list","Its formatting is kept as plain formatting, but the style name isn't","The text is deleted","It becomes Heading 1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Google Docs keeps the look of the text but can't keep a custom style name."},{"q":"Which key combination shows formatting marks in Word for Windows?","options":["Ctrl+Shift+8","Ctrl+8","Alt+8","Ctrl+Alt+8"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ctrl+Shift+8 (the * key) toggles the ¶ marks for paragraphs, spaces and breaks."}],"exercise":{"title":"Round trip the report","task":"<p>Take your styled report from the first lesson and open it in the other program. Check the title, headings and any table of contents, then export a PDF.</p>","files":["report-draft.docx"],"solution":["From Word to Google Docs: File &gt; Open &gt; Upload in Google Docs. From Google Docs to Word: File &gt; Download &gt; Microsoft Word (.docx).","Check that the title still uses Title and each heading uses Heading 1 or 2 (click in it and look at the style drop-down or gallery).","Update the table of contents if there is one.","Export a PDF and page through it to check the layout."]}}],"versionNote":"Software makers update their programs, so menus and button locations can change between versions. This guide is revised when an update changes the steps, and the revised version appears the next time you open it."};
